$ dorc --book=book.sh --pre-source chroot.oracle.sh --pre-source kp.oracle.sh --results - < probe-results.txt
reserved: warning[reserved-namespace-squat]: Book function `kp__is_converged`
sits in the `__is_converged` namespace reserved for oracle functions; Dorc
treats it as ordinary shell and runs it verbatim, but a shipped oracle preamble
defining the same name would collide with it. Rename it to stay clear of
`*__is_converged`.
      [ book.sh:22:1
   22 | kp__is_converged() {
      | \______________/
oracle: warning[oracle-matched-zero-sites]: [unwritten:
oracle-matched-zero-sites]
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=149516e32ef05de6e9710a06881d0e031f220d5041a98cdb53929b8e1323b067 sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
classify: note[cmdsub-operand-top]: operand 2 is an unresolvable positional
parameter (`$@`/`$*`/`$N`), so Dorc cannot know its value until the command runs
on the host -- there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe to check. Dorc
elides a command only when it can prove the command's effect is already in
place, so this one is left to run on every apply.
      [ book.sh:24:13
   24 |    kp check "$1" "$want"   : sm.dorc.KernelParam:"$1"
      |             \__/

   = repair:  Give the operand a value Dorc can resolve statically -- a literal,
              or a variable assigned from one -- so Dorc can probe it and elide
              the command once it has converged. If the value must stay dynamic,
              load an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and
              Dorc will guard the command instead of running it every time.
classify: note[cmdsub-inner-nonleaf]: Dorc cannot elide `cat /etc/kp-policy` on
its own: it runs inside a `$(...)` substitution as part of evaluating its
enclosing line, so it runs whenever that line does.
      [ book.sh:1:1
   1 | #!/bin/sh
     | \_______/
   2 | # dorc-lang/v0.2
     | \______/
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 1 site run unprobed (no read-only check could
be shipped): `chroot /mnt kp ip_forward 1` -- run `dorc why` for the per-site
detail (the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:26:1
   26 | chroot /mnt kp ip_forward 1
      | \_________________________/

   = help:  sm site runs `chroot /mnt kp ip_forward 1`
why: ran because operand 2 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 24:4 `kp check "$1" "$want"   : sm.dorc.KernelParam:"$1"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
hint: 'chroot' (line 26) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
